  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You've been working with subarrays for so long that you're sick of them – subarrays are now banned!\n\nFormally, consider a set of indices {i1,i2,…,ik}\\{i_1, i_2, \\ldots, i_k\\}{i1​,i2​,…,ik​} such that 1≤i1&lt;i2&lt;⋯&lt;ik≤N1 \\leq i_1 \\lt i_2 \\lt \\cdots \\lt i_k \\leq N1≤i1​&lt;i2​&lt;⋯&lt;ik​≤N.\n\nThis set is said to be an anti-subarray if ik−i1&gt;k−1i_k - i_1 \\gt k - 1ik​−i1​&gt;k−1.\n\nIn particular, note that an anti-subarray must have length greater than 111.\n\nYou have an array AAA containing NNN (N≥4N \\geq 4N≥4) integers, a boolean array BBB of length NNN, and an integer XXX.\n\nYou can perform the following operation:\n\n\nChoose an index iii (1≤i≤N1 \\leq i \\leq N1≤i≤N) such that Bi=1B_i = 1Bi​=1.\nThen, increase AiA_iAi​ by 111.\nFind the minimum number of operations needed to obtain an anti-subarray whose sum is **at least** XXX.\n\nIt is guaranteed that BBB contains at least one element equal to 111.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nEach test case consists of three lines of input.\n\nThe first line of each test case contains two space-separated integers NNN and XXX – the length of the array and the target sum.\nThe second line contains NNN space-separated integers A1,A2,…,ANA_1, A_2, \\ldots, A_NA1​,A2​,…,AN​.\nThe third line contains NNN space-separated integers B1,B2,…,BNB_1, B_2, \\ldots, B_NB1​,B2​,…,BN​.\n\n\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line a single integer: the minimum number of operations needed to obtain an anti-subset of AAA with sum ≥X\\geq X≥X.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1051 \\leq T \\leq 10^51≤T≤105",
      "4≤N≤2⋅1054 \\leq N \\leq 2\\cdot 10^54≤N≤2⋅105",
      "−109≤Ai≤109-10^9 \\leq A_i \\leq 10^9−109≤Ai​≤109",
      "−2⋅1014≤X≤2⋅1014-2\\cdot 10^{14} \\leq X \\leq 2\\cdot 10^{14}−2⋅1014≤X≤2⋅1014",
      "Bi∈{0,1}B_i \\in \\{0, 1\\}Bi​∈{0,1}, and at least one element of BBB equals 111.",
      "The sum of NNN over all test cases won't exceed 2⋅1052\\cdot 10^52⋅105."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "4\n4 9\n-200 5 100 2\n1 1 1 1\n4 9\n-200 5 100 2\n0 0 1 0\n5 100\n-28 40 -63 39 -109\n1 0 1 0 1\n5 70\n-28 40 -63 39 -109\n1 0 1 0 1",
        "output": "2\n107\n49\n0",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: We're allowed to increase any element. One optimal solution is:\n\nIncrease A2A_2A2​ by 111, to obtain [−200,6,100,2][-200, 6, 100, 2][−200,6,100,2].\nIncrease A4A_4A4​ by 111, to obtain [−200,6,100,3][-200, 6, 100, 3][−200,6,100,3].\nNow there's an anti-subarray with sum ≥9\\geq 9≥9, formed by indices {2,4}\\{2, 4\\}{2,4}.\n\nTest case 222: Same array and target as the previous test, but now only A3A_3A3​ can be increased.\nIncreasing it 107107107 times results in [−200,5,207,2][-200, 5, 207, 2][−200,5,207,2], which has the anti-subarray with indices {1,3,4}\\{1, 3, 4\\}{1,3,4} giving a sum of −200+207+2=9-200 + 207 + 2 = 9−200+207+2=9, which is the best we can do.\nTest case 444: There's already an anti-subarray with sum ≥X=70\\geq X = 70≥X=70, so no operations are needed."
      }
    ]
